      "problem": "By expressing $\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)$ in the form $k\\ln x,$ determine the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k =2.$",
